cleared. But the base Formatter.format does record.message = record.getMessage(). With copy, fine.\n\nBut base formatter format() also does exc_text caching; fine on copy.\n\nApproach in NewLineFormatter.format:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = _truncate_message(message, max_chars)\n        if truncated != message:\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = None  # or ()\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\n\nNote args = None: getMessage does `if self.args: msg = msg % self.args` → None is falsy, fine. But careful: the record copy still has args original? We set args=None. But the message we computed after % substitution. Good.\n\nHowever: careful with `record.msg` being non-str? When args present, `str(self.msg)`. Our truncated is str. Fine.\n\nCareful: record.getMessage() may raise if args mismatch? It's the base behavior anyway; base format calls getMessage too. Slight double computation, fine.\n\nTruncation helper:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    budget = max_chars - len(_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    if budget <= 0:\n        return _OMITTED_MARKER[:max_chars]  # shouldn't happen\n    head_len = (budget + 1) // 2  # ceil\n    tail_len = budget - head_len\n    head = message[:head_len]\n    tail = message[len(message) - tail_len:] if tail_len > 0 else \"\"\n    return f\"{head}{_OMITTED_MARKER}{tail}\"\n```\n\nCheck example budget 32: marker len = 15 (\"...<omitted>...\" = 3+9+3 = 15). budget = 17. head = 9, tail = 8. ✓ matches.\n\nCareful with slice `message[len(message)-tail_len:]` when tail_len==0 → message[len:] = \"\" ok actually message[len(message):] = \"